data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"data\": {\n    \"lookupStatus\": \"found\",\n    \"posts\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1112596418425067\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/groups/118833031134749/permalink/1112596418425067/\",\n        \"text\": \"**Parents, who is doing the recruiting?**\\n\\n                                                                                                             I coached for 46 years, and I see the same thing over and over now that I work with families on recruiting. It starts out fine. Mom or Dad helps with the school list, then they're writing the emails, and by the time the visit comes they're the ones asking all the questions while the swimmer stands off to the side saying nothing. \\n\\n                                                                                                     Coaches pick up on that quickly. If the kid never says a word, the coach has no idea whether she even wants to be there, and that matters when they're deciding who to offer a spot to.\\n\\n                                                                                                          So let your swimmer write the first email. It might be a little rough, and that's okay. Let them ask their own questions on the visit, even if you thought of a better one in the car. Your job is to keep an eye on the deadlines, be straight with them about what college is going to cost, and look over the list with them.\\n\\n                                                                                                        The kids who pick the school themselves are the ones who hang in there when practice is miserable and it's February and they're homesick.\\n\\nIf you find yourself in this situation, contact me and one of our advisors can set up a no obligation Zoom call to find out if we can help you.\\n\\n***kevin@accrecruits.com***\",\n        \"publishedAt\": \"2026-11-05T23:05:42.000Z\",\n        \"reactionCount\": 13,\n        \"commentCount\": 3,\n        \"isPaidPartnership\": null,\n        \"sharedPostId\": null,\n        \"sharedPostUrl\": null,\n        \"author\": {\n          \"id\": \"pfbid02tzihdFDZ3PSnhiSAihqM1N2zpdQgeswdQHv2kFmXPZBaa1LT8XKkYc8GowjUdhjxl\",\n          \"name\": \"Kevin Timothy Weldon\",\n          \"url\": null\n        },\n        \"video\": {\n          \"thumbnailUrl\": null,\n          \"durationMs\": null,\n          \"sdUrl\": null,\n          \"hdUrl\": null\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1112292218455487\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/groups/118833031134749/permalink/1112292218455487/\",\n        \"text\": \"College recruiting can quietly turn into a parent's project rather than a swimmer's journey.\\n\\nIt starts with the best intentions: researching programmes, drafting emails, tracking deadlines. But before long, the swimmer is following a plan they did not really choose, answering questions their parents prepared, and wondering whether the whole process is theirs at all.\\n\\nWhat helps is keeping the swimmer in the driving seat, even when it would be quicker to do it for them. Let them write the first email, even if it is imperfect. Let them ask the coach their own questions on a visit. Parents can still be the safety net — checking deadlines, talking through options, being honest about cost — without holding the pen.\\n\\nA swimmer who chooses their own college is far more likely to thrive there, through the hard weeks as well as the good ones.\\n\\nHow have you balanced helping with letting your swimmer lead?\",\n        \"publishedAt\": \"2026-11-05T15:51:48.000Z\",\n        \"reactionCount\": 3,\n        \"commentCount\": 12,\n        \"isPaidPartnership\": null,\n        \"sharedPostId\": null,\n        \"sharedPostUrl\": null,\n        \"author\": {\n          \"id\": \"61594176031635\",\n          \"name\": \"Andrew Pace\",\n          \"url\": null\n        },\n        \"video\": {\n          \"thumbnailUrl\": null,\n          \"durationMs\": null,\n          \"sdUrl\": null,\n          \"hdUrl\": null\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1110759795275396\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/groups/118833031134749/permalink/1110759795275396/\",\n        \"text\": \"My swimmer class of 28 was offered a tour by the swim coach at a D3 school. What questions should we be asking? I’m going to let my swimmer take the lead but I feel unprepared!\",\n        \"publishedAt\": \"2026-11-04T04:49:31.000Z\",\n        \"reactionCount\": 8,\n        \"commentCount\": 6,\n        \"isPaidPartnership\": null,\n        \"sharedPostId\": null,\n        \"sharedPostUrl\": null,\n        \"author\": {\n          \"id\": \"29260437006902338\",\n          \"name\": \"CollegeNJ2728\",\n          \"url\": null\n        },\n        \"video\": {\n          \"thumbnailUrl\": null,\n          \"durationMs\": null,\n          \"sdUrl\": null,\n          \"hdUrl\": null\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1110319101986132\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/groups/118833031134749/permalink/1110319101986132/\",\n        \"text\": \"My swimmer is applying ED to a high academic D3 with coach support. It's very likely he will be accepted. He applied to a couple backup schools early action just in case. Should he let the coaches know he applied at the early action schools? He is hesitant because he has accepted an offer from the ED school.\",\n        \"publishedAt\": \"2026-11-03T17:44:23.000Z\",\n        \"reactionCount\": 3,\n        \"commentCount\": 16,\n        \"isPaidPartnership\": null,\n        \"sharedPostId\": null,\n        \"sharedPostUrl\": null,\n        \"author\": {\n          \"id\": \"983360734268907\",\n          \"name\": \"DelightfulFlower9497\",\n          \"url\": null\n        },\n        \"video\": {\n          \"thumbnailUrl\": null,\n          \"durationMs\": null,\n          \"sdUrl\": null,\n          \"hdUrl\": null\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"page\": {\n      \"nextCursor\": \"AQHT-3QtGOFSNE3B5rgHGif-aFvI-MvYCtj-foYT98NqDO83LoYjdUgaFrtsKCaRPBNCjcdnmTsLYSFGo0_x2S7fXg:eyIwIjoxNzkzOTgxOTQ5LCIxIjo3NjgyLCIzIjoxLCI0IjoxLCI1IjoyLCI2IjotMSwiNyI6LTEsIjgiOi0xfQ==\",\n      \"hasMore\": true\n    }\n  },\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"requestId\": \"req_b168f1a8-7e17-403f-b4e8-c0de737717b0\",\n    \"creditsCharged\": 1,\n    \"version\": \"v1\"\n  }\n}"},{"type":"text","text":"{\"conversation_id\":\"01a1b1a8-22f6-7ed2-8e58-b7510eda4dd9\"}"}],"structuredContent":{"data":{"lookupStatus":"found","posts":[{"id":"1112596418425067","url":"https://www.facebook.com/groups/118833031134749/permalink/1112596418425067/","text":"**Parents, who is doing the recruiting?**\n\n                                                                                                             I coached for 46 years, and I see the same thing over and over now that I work with families on recruiting. It starts out fine. Mom or Dad helps with the school list, then they're writing the emails, and by the time the visit comes they're the ones asking all the questions while the swimmer stands off to the side saying nothing. \n\n                                                                                                     Coaches pick up on that quickly. If the kid never says a word, the coach has no idea whether she even wants to be there, and that matters when they're deciding who to offer a spot to.\n\n                                                                                                          So let your swimmer write the first email. It might be a little rough, and that's okay. Let them ask their own questions on the visit, even if you thought of a better one in the car. Your job is to keep an eye on the deadlines, be straight with them about what college is going to cost, and look over the list with them.\n\n                                                                                                        The kids who pick the school themselves are the ones who hang in there when practice is miserable and it's February and they're homesick.\n\nIf you find yourself in this situation, contact me and one of our advisors can set up a no obligation Zoom call to find out if we can help you.\n\n***kevin@accrecruits.com***","publishedAt":"2026-11-05T23:05:42.000Z","reactionCount":13,"commentCount":3,"isPaidPartnership":null,"sharedPostId":null,"sharedPostUrl":null,"author":{"id":"pfbid02tzihdFDZ3PSnhiSAihqM1N2zpdQgeswdQHv2kFmXPZBaa1LT8XKkYc8GowjUdhjxl","name":"Kevin Timothy Weldon","url":null},"video":{"thumbnailUrl":null,"durationMs":null,"sdUrl":null,"hdUrl":null}},{"id":"1112292218455487","url":"https://www.facebook.com/groups/118833031134749/permalink/1112292218455487/","text":"College recruiting can quietly turn into a parent's project rather than a swimmer's journey.\n\nIt starts with the best intentions: researching programmes, drafting emails, tracking deadlines. But before long, the swimmer is following a plan they did not really choose, answering questions their parents prepared, and wondering whether the whole process is theirs at all.\n\nWhat helps is keeping the swimmer in the driving seat, even when it would be quicker to do it for them. Let them write the first email, even if it is imperfect. Let them ask the coach their own questions on a visit. Parents can still be the safety net — checking deadlines, talking through options, being honest about cost — without holding the pen.\n\nA swimmer who chooses their own college is far more likely to thrive there, through the hard weeks as well as the good ones.\n\nHow have you balanced helping with letting your swimmer lead?","publishedAt":"2026-11-05T15:51:48.000Z","reactionCount":3,"commentCount":12,"isPaidPartnership":null,"sharedPostId":null,"sharedPostUrl":null,"author":{"id":"61594176031635","name":"Andrew Pace","url":null},"video":{"thumbnailUrl":null,"durationMs":null,"sdUrl":n